04 — TRANSITIONS & MOTION DESIGN

Create a consistent motion system for the entire application.

Animations must be subtle, fast, smooth, and useful.

Do not use exaggerated animations.

Do not animate every element unnecessarily.

PAGE TRANSITIONS

Use smooth transitions when entering or leaving major application sections.

Recommended duration:

* 160–220ms for small UI transitions.
* 200–280ms for section transitions.
* 250–350ms for modal and bottom-sheet animations.

Use subtle opacity transitions, slight movement, and carefully controlled easing.

BOTTOM SHEETS

Use bottom sheets for contextual actions, selections, filters, and short forms whenever they improve the experience.

Examples:

* Vehicle selection.
* Delivery details.
* Address selection.
* Shipment actions.
* Filters.
* Payment method selection.
* Confirmation details.

Bottom sheets must:

* Open smoothly.
* Have clear visual hierarchy.
* Be dismissible when appropriate.
* Adapt to screen height.
* Support scrolling when content exceeds available space.
* Preserve the underlying context.
* Avoid unnecessary full-page navigation.

BUTTON INTERACTIONS

Buttons must provide immediate feedback.

Use:

* Press states.
* Subtle scale or opacity changes.
* Loading states.
* Disabled states.
* Success feedback.
* Error feedback.

Do not allow users to repeatedly submit the same action while it is processing.

LIST INTERACTIONS

Lists should feel responsive.

Use subtle appearance transitions, smooth filtering, and clear selection states.

MAP INTERACTIONS

Map movements must feel natural.

Avoid unnecessarily resetting the map when switching between related delivery steps.

REDUCED MOTION

Respect operating system accessibility preferences for reduced motion.

